'''''Oscularia''''' is a [[genus]] of succulent [[flowering plant]]s in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Aizoaceae, [[native plant|native]] to semi-arid and rocky habitats in the [[Western Cape]] of [[South Africa]].

It was previously included within the related genus ''[[Lampranthus]]'', but was split off as a separate genus, due to differences in its fruit and flower.

==Description==
[[File:Oscularia caulescens WPC.jpg|thumb|right|''[[Oscularia caulescens]]'']]
The most superficially recognisable feature of the genus is the shape of the leaves, which are grey-green and waxy. They are triangular in cross-section (3 angled) and can be sickle, club or mouth shaped. The name ''"Oscularia"'' actually means ''"group of tiny mouths"'' in Latin, and refers to the appearance of the toothed leaves in some species.{{citation needed|date=February 2021}} The stems are often red, and the leaves can become red too during times of drought.

Abundant, almond-scented, daisy-like white or pink flowers appear throughout the summer.<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|pages=1136}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://gateway.myspecies.info/oscularia | title=Oscularia &#124; GATEWAY TO AFRICAN PLANTS }}</ref>

==Distribution==
The species are restricted to the [[Western Cape]] Province, [[South Africa]], where they occur only in winter rainfall areas. Their habitat is typically rocky areas of sandstone.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.plantzafrica.com/plantnop/osculdelt.htm |title=Oscularia deltoides (L.) L.Bolus|website=plantzafrica.com |access-date=2015-01-14 |archive-date=2015-09-24 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924114650/http://www.plantzafrica.com/plantnop/osculdelt.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The species ''[[Oscularia deltoides]]'' is cultivated as an ornamental garden plant.

